No rate returns our own bytes. Note 19200 and 28800: the first three received
bytes are identical for both parities, so the same waveform is arriving and
only the decoder changes. Note 115200/125000: the reply repeats at the TX
*frame* rate with the 0101 pattern inside each byte gone entirely.

=== BITBANG PULSE RESPONSE ==================================================
Synchronous bitbang, driving each candidate pin in turn and sampling RXD on
the same clock. Run at two sample clocks, 4800 and 115200.

  TXD  pulse/64, pulse/160, square/8, square/32 : RXD did NOT follow
  RTS  pulse/64, pulse/160, square/32           : RXD did NOT follow
  RTS  square/8 at the slow clock only          : RXD low in 27 samples
  DTR  all shapes                               : RXD did NOT follow

The single RTS hit did not reproduce at the faster clock and is treated as
noise. This is a null result that is NOT fully explained: in UART mode the
line demonstrably responds, and in bitbang mode it does not. Possibly the
transceiver's enable depends on chip state that bitbang mode changes. Noted
rather than interpreted.
